    <article id="module1" class="card">
      <h2>Module 1: CSS Fundamentals</h2>
      <section id="module-1-1">
        <h3>Introduction to CSS</h3>
        <p>CSS (Cascading Style Sheets) controls how HTML content looks &mdash; colors, spacing, typography, layout &mdash; while HTML stays focused purely on structure and meaning. That separation is the whole point: change one stylesheet, and every page that links to it updates together.</p>
        <p>There are three ways to attach CSS to a page: <strong>inline</strong>, directly on an element via the <code>style</code> attribute; <strong>internal</strong>, inside a <code>&lt;style&gt;</code> block in the document <code>&lt;head&gt;</code>; and <strong>external</strong>, in a separate <code>.css</code> file linked with <code>&lt;link rel="stylesheet"&gt;</code>. Developer Xperience uses the external approach &mdash; every page on this site, including this one, links to a single <code>style.css</code> file, which is why a change to one stylesheet reshapes the whole platform at once.</p>
        <p>When styles from more than one source apply to the same element, the browser resolves the conflict through <strong>the cascade</strong>: inline styles win over internal and external ones regardless of order, and among internal/external rules, the more specific selector wins &mdash; or, if equally specific, whichever rule appears later in the file. Some properties, like <code>color</code> and <code>font-family</code>, are also <strong>inherited</strong> automatically from parent to child unless overridden; others, like <code>margin</code> and <code>border</code>, are not.</p>
      </section>
      <section id="module-1-2">
        <h3>CSS Syntax and Selectors</h3>
        <p>A CSS rule is a selector paired with a declaration block:</p>
        <pre><code>selector {
  property: value;
          }</code></pre>
        <p>The selector decides which elements the rule targets. The most common types: an <strong>element</strong> selector matches every instance of a tag (<code>p</code>, <code>h2</code>); a <strong>class</strong> selector (<code>.card</code>) matches any element with that class, and can be reused across many elements; an <strong>id</strong> selector (<code>#main-content</code>) matches exactly one element, since ids must be unique on a page; and the <strong>universal</strong> selector (<code>*</code>) matches everything &mdash; Developer Xperience's own reset uses it: <code>*, *::before, *::after { box-sizing: border-box; }</code>. Selectors can also be <strong>grouped</strong> with commas to share a declaration block, exactly like this site's own <code>h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6 { font-weight: 700; }</code> rule.</p>
        <p>Interactive selectors &mdash; <code>:hover</code>, <code>:focus</code>, <code>:nth-child()</code>, and the rest of the pseudo-class family &mdash; get their own full treatment in Module 3.</p>
      </section>
      <section id="module-1-3">
        <h3>Box Model</h3>
        <p>Every element renders as a rectangular box made of four layers, from the inside out: <strong>content</strong>, <strong>padding</strong>, <strong>border</strong>, and <strong>margin</strong>. By default (<code>box-sizing: content-box</code>), the <code>width</code> and <code>height</code> you set apply only to the content &mdash; padding and border get added on top, so a 200px-wide box with 20px padding and a 2px border actually renders 244px wide. That's a common source of layout surprises.</p>
        <p><code>box-sizing: border-box</code> changes the math so <code>width</code>/<code>height</code> include padding and border instead, making sizing far more predictable &mdash; which is exactly why Developer Xperience's global reset sets <code>box-sizing: border-box</code> on every element (<code>*, *::before, *::after</code>) right at the top of <code>style.css</code>. Margin always sits outside the box, controlling the space between elements rather than the element's own size.</p>
      </section>
      <section id="module-1-4">
        <h3>Colors and Backgrounds</h3>
        <p>CSS accepts color in several formats: named colors (<code>navy</code>), hex (<code>#1a1a1f</code> &mdash; the exact shade Developer Xperience uses for <code>--card</code>), <code>rgb()</code>/<code>rgba()</code> for red-green-blue values with optional transparency, and <code>hsl()</code>/<code>hsla()</code> for hue-saturation-lightness, often the most intuitive format for hand-tweaking a color's brightness or intensity.</p>
        <p>Backgrounds go beyond flat color: <code>background-image</code>, <code>background-position</code>, <code>background-size</code>, and <code>background-attachment</code> combine to control how an image or gradient fills an element. This site's <code>body</code> layers two soft radial gradients over a solid base color:</p>
        <pre><code>background-image:
  radial-gradient(1200px 600px at 80% -10%, rgba(0, 212, 170, 0.12), transparent 60%),
          radial-gradient(900px 500px at -10% 10%, rgba(66, 150, 250, 0.1), transparent 55%);</code></pre>
        <p>Multiple backgrounds are simply comma-separated, painted in the order listed &mdash; the first one ends up on top.</p>
      </section>
      <section id="module-1-5">
        <h3>Typography</h3>
        <p>Typography properties shape how text reads: <code>font-family</code> (with fallback fonts, like this site's <code>"Segoe UI", Tahoma, Geneva, Verdana, sans-serif</code> stack), <code>font-size</code>, <code>font-weight</code>, <code>font-style</code>, <code>line-height</code>, <code>letter-spacing</code>, and plain <code>color</code> for text color. <code>text-align</code>, <code>text-decoration</code>, and <code>text-transform</code> control alignment and casing rather than the font itself. When a design needs a typeface beyond the system stack, <code>@font-face</code> (or a service like Google Fonts) loads one.</p>
        <p>Rather than hardcoding the same values everywhere, Developer Xperience defines its colors and spacing once as custom properties &mdash; <code>--bg</code>, <code>--card</code>, <code>--border</code>, <code>--accent</code>, <code>--text</code>, and so on &mdash; and every rule in <code>style.css</code> reuses those variables. That's a design-system habit worth adopting early: pick a palette, name it, and reuse it.</p>
      </section>
      <section id="module-1-6">
        <h3>Links and Navigation</h3>
        <p>Links carry four state-based pseudo-classes, conventionally written in this order &mdash; nicknamed <strong>LVHA</strong> &mdash; because later rules override earlier ones for elements that match more than one: <code>:link</code> (unvisited), <code>:visited</code> (already visited), <code>:hover</code> (pointer over it), and <code>:active</code> (mid-click). <code>:focus</code>/<code>:focus-visible</code> covers keyboard navigation and sits logically alongside <code>:hover</code>.</p>
        <p>Developer Xperience keeps its link styling minimal and consistent: an accent color, no underline, and a smooth color transition on <code>:hover</code>/<code>:focus</code>, defined once on the bare <code>a</code> selector in <code>style.css</code> and inherited by every link on the site unless a more specific rule (like <code>.main-navigation a</code>) overrides it.</p>
      </section>
      <section id="module-1-8">
        <h3>Sample Code</h3>
        <p>CSS rule syntax, attaching CSS, and the box model:</p>
        <pre><code>/* 1. Rule syntax: selector + declaration block */
.card {
  padding: 1.5rem;
  border: 2px solid #3a3a4a;
}

/* 2. Attaching CSS - external stylesheet keeps styles in one place */
&lt;link rel="stylesheet" href="assets/css/style.css"&gt;

/* 3. Box model: content, padding, border, margin */
.box {
  width: 200px;           /* content width */
  padding: 20px;          /* inside the border */
  border: 2px solid navy; /* the edge */
  margin: 10px;           /* outside the border */
  box-sizing: border-box; /* width includes padding + border */
          }</code></pre>
      </section>
      <section id="module-1-7">
        <h3>Summary</h3>
        <p>Module 1 covered how CSS attaches to a page and cascades, how selectors target elements, the box model, color and background formats, typography basics, and link states. Together these are the vocabulary every other module builds on &mdash; Module 2 moves from styling individual elements to arranging them into full layouts.</p>
        <table border="1">
          <caption>CSS Fundamentals at a Glance</caption>
