      </section>
    </article>
    <hr />
  <article id="module2" class="card">
    <h2>Module 2: CSS Layout</h2>
    <section id="module-2-1">
      <h3>Positioning</h3>
      <p>The <code>position</code> property changes how an element participates in layout: <code>static</code> (the default &mdash; normal document flow, <code>top</code>/<code>left</code>/etc. have no effect), <code>relative</code> (shifted from its normal spot, but the space it originally occupied is preserved), <code>absolute</code> (removed from flow entirely, positioned against its nearest ancestor that has a <code>position</code> other than <code>static</code>), <code>fixed</code> (positioned against the viewport, so it stays put on scroll), and <code>sticky</code> (behaves like <code>relative</code> until a scroll threshold, then locks like <code>fixed</code>). <code>z-index</code> only affects elements that are positioned (anything other than <code>static</code>), controlling which one stacks on top when they overlap.</p>
    </section>
    <section id="module-2-2">
      <h3>Flexbox</h3>
      <p>Flexbox arranges items along a single line &mdash; a row or a column &mdash; set with <code>display: flex</code> on the container. <code>justify-content</code> positions items along that main axis; <code>align-items</code> positions them along the cross axis; individual items can grow, shrink, or hold a starting size with <code>flex-grow</code>, <code>flex-shrink</code>, and <code>flex-basis</code>.</p>
      <p>Developer Xperience's own header banner is a working Flexbox example:</p>
      <pre><code>.banner {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  gap: 1.5rem;
  align-items: center;
        }</code></pre>
      <p><code>flex-wrap: wrap</code> lets the image and heading drop to a second line instead of overflowing on narrow screens, and <code>gap</code> handles the spacing between them without needing margin on every item.</p>
    </section>
    <section id="module-2-3">
      <h3>Grid</h3>
      <p>CSS Grid lays things out in two dimensions at once &mdash; rows and columns together &mdash; starting with <code>display: grid</code> on the container. <code>grid-template-columns</code> and <code>grid-template-rows</code> define the tracks; the <code>fr</code> unit divides remaining space proportionally; <code>grid-template-areas</code> lets you name regions and place items by name instead of by number.</p>
      <p>The module sections on this page are Grid in action:</p>
      <pre><code>.module-layout {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: 1fr;
  gap: 2rem;
  align-items: start;
        }</code></pre>
      <p>A single <code>1fr</code> track stacks the module sections in one column that spans the full viewport width at every screen size &mdash; the same full-width pattern used across this site. Add more <code>1fr</code> tracks (say, <code>repeat(2, 1fr)</code> for an even two-column split) and a media query to collapse them when you want a multi-column layout of your own.</p>
    </section>
    <section id="module-2-4">
      <h3>Responsive Design</h3>
      <p>Responsive design means one set of markup adapts across phone, tablet, and desktop screens rather than shipping separate versions. It rests on a few pieces working together: the viewport meta tag (<code>&lt;meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0"&gt;</code>, already in every page's <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> on this site) tells mobile browsers to render at device width instead of zoomed out; relative units and flexible layouts (Flexbox, Grid) let content reflow naturally; and media queries step in for the adjustments flexible layout alone can't handle.</p>
    </section>
    <section id="module-2-5">
      <h3>Media Queries</h3>
      <p>A media query applies a block of CSS only when a condition &mdash; most often screen width &mdash; is true:</p>
      <pre><code>@media (max-width: 768px) {
  /* styles that only apply at 768px and narrower */
        }</code></pre>
      <p>Developer Xperience uses exactly this pattern, with breakpoints at 768px and 600px, to shrink card padding, stack the header banner, let wide tables scroll horizontally, and tighten spacing on small screens. Beyond width, media features can also target things like orientation, resolution, or user preferences &mdash; this site's <code>@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)</code> block is a media query too, just testing a preference instead of a screen size.</p>
    </section>
    <section id="module-2-7">
      <h3>Sample Code</h3>
      <p>One example of each layout tool from this module:</p>
      <pre><code>/* Positioning: sticky header that stays on scroll */
.site-header {
  position: sticky;
  top: 0;
  z-index: 10;
}

/* Flexbox: navigation that wraps on narrow screens */
.nav {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  gap: 1rem;
  justify-content: center;
}

/* Grid: responsive columns without a media query */
.gallery {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(180px, 1fr));
  gap: 1rem;
}

/* Media query: single column below 600px */
@media (max-width: 600px) {
  .gallery {
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
  }
        }</code></pre>
    </section>
    <section id="module-2-6">
      <h3>Summary</h3>
      <p>Module 2 moved from styling single elements to arranging whole pages: positioning takes elements out of or within normal flow, Flexbox and Grid handle one- and two-dimensional layout, and media queries adapt all of it to the screen it's rendered on. Module 3 builds on this foundation with interactivity &mdash; states, transitions, and animation.</p>
      <table border="1">
        <caption>CSS Layout Tools at a Glance</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Concept</th>
            <th scope="col">Category</th>
            <th scope="col">What It Does</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>position</td>
            <td>Layout</td>
            <td>static, relative, absolute, fixed, sticky - each leaves or keeps flow differently</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Flexbox</td>
            <td>Layout</td>
            <td>One-dimensional rows or columns; justify-content and align-items</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Grid</td>
            <td>Layout</td>
            <td>Two-dimensional rows and columns; fr units and template areas</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Responsive design</td>
            <td>Approach</td>
            <td>One markup set adapting to any screen via flexible layouts</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Media queries</td>
            <td>Approach</td>
            <td>Apply CSS only when a condition (usually width) is true</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </section>
  </article>
  <hr />
<article id="module3" class="card">
  <h2>Module 3: CSS Interactivity</h2>
  <section id="module-3-1">
    <h3>Pseudo-classes and Pseudo-elements</h3>
    <p><strong>Pseudo-classes</strong> select elements based on state or position rather than tag, class, or id: <code>:hover</code>, <code>:focus</code>, <code>:active</code>, <code>:visited</code> (state), or <code>:first-child</code>, <code>:last-child</code>, <code>:nth-child(n)</code> (position among siblings). <strong>Pseudo-elements</strong> &mdash; written with a double colon, <code>::before</code> and <code>::after</code> &mdash; insert generated content into an element without adding a real DOM node, commonly used for decorative marks, subtle dividers, or clearing floats.</p>
    <pre><code>.card::before {
  content: "&rarr; ";
  color: var(--accent);
      }</code></pre>
    <p>The distinction is worth keeping straight: pseudo-<em>classes</em> target something that already exists (a state, a position); pseudo-<em>elements</em> create something that didn't exist in the markup.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="module-3-2">
    <h3>Transitions and Animations</h3>
    <p>A <strong>transition</strong> smoothly interpolates a property between two values over time, triggered by a state change like <code>:hover</code>. Developer Xperience uses this constantly &mdash; links, nav items, and cards all define something like <code>transition: color 0.2s ease, background-color 0.2s ease</code> so hover states fade in rather than snapping instantly.</p>
    <p><strong>Animations</strong> go further: <code>@keyframes</code> defines a named sequence of states, and the <code>animation</code> shorthand (name, duration, timing function, delay, iteration count) plays it &mdash; with or without a trigger, and it can loop indefinitely. Reach for a transition when smoothing a single state change; reach for an animation when a effect needs multiple steps or should run on its own.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="module-3-3">
    <h3>Transforms</h3>
    <p><code>transform</code> repositions, resizes, rotates, or skews an element visually &mdash; <code>translate()</code>, <code>scale()</code>, <code>rotate()</code>, <code>skew()</code> &mdash; without affecting the document's layout flow, which is exactly why transforms are so cheap to animate: the browser doesn't have to recalculate everyone else's position. <code>transform-origin</code> sets the pivot point transforms are measured from; the default is the element's own center. Pair a transform with a transition and a hover state for a classic, low-cost lift-and-scale card effect.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="module-3-4">
    <h3>Forms Styling</h3>
    <p>Form elements (<code>input</code>, <code>select</code>, <code>textarea</code>, <code>button</code>) don't inherit typography the way most elements do by default, which is why Developer Xperience's stylesheet explicitly resets it &mdash; <code>font-family: inherit; font-size: inherit;</code> on <code>button</code>. Beyond typography, styling forms usually means custom borders, padding, and clear <code>:focus</code>/<code>:disabled</code>/invalid states so users always know what a field is doing. Grouping related fields with <code>&lt;fieldset&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;legend&gt;</code> keeps both the visual layout and the accessibility tree organized together.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="module-3-5">
    <h3>Focus States</h3>
    <p><code>:focus-visible</code> is the modern refinement of <code>:focus</code> &mdash; it shows a focus ring for keyboard navigation but stays quiet on a mouse click, so mouse users don't see an outline they don't need while keyboard users still get one. Developer Xperience applies this site-wide:</p>
    <pre><code>a:focus-visible {
  outline: 2px solid var(--accent);
  outline-offset: 2px;
      }</code></pre>
    <p>The stylesheet also includes a skip link (<code>.content-link</code>), hidden off-screen until it receives keyboard focus, at which point it snaps into view so a keyboard user can jump straight to <code>#main-content</code> without tabbing through the entire navigation first. Never remove an outline with <code>outline: none</code> unless replacing it with an equally visible alternative &mdash; Developer Xperience's own focus rules are exactly that replacement.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="module-3-7">
    <h3>Sample Code</h3>
    <p>Interactivity in practice &mdash; pseudo-classes, transitions, transforms, and focus:</p>
    <pre><code>/* Pseudo-class and pseudo-element */
.nav a:hover {
  color: var(--accent);
}

.card::before {
  content: "\2192 ";
  color: var(--accent);
}

/* Transition + transform: smooth hover lift */
.card {
  transition: transform 0.3s ease, box-shadow 0.3s ease;
}

.card:hover {
  transform: translateY(-4px);
  box-shadow: 0 12px 40px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.55);
}

/* Accessible focus ring for keyboard users */
a:focus-visible {
  outline: 2px solid var(--accent);
  outline-offset: 2px;
      }</code></pre>
  </section>
  <section id="module-3-6">
    <h3>Summary</h3>
    <p>Module 3 added interactivity without JavaScript: pseudo-classes and pseudo-elements react to state, transitions and animations add motion, transforms reposition elements cheaply, and accessible focus and form styling keep all of it usable for keyboard and screen-reader users too. Module 4 picks up more advanced layout tools &mdash; variables, math functions, and subgrid &mdash; building on everything so far.</p>
