<article id="module4" class="card">
  <h2>Module 4: Advanced Layout Techniques</h2>
  <section id="module-4-1">
    <h3>CSS Variables</h3>
    <p>Custom properties &mdash; CSS's version of variables &mdash; are declared with a double-dash prefix and read with <code>var()</code>. Developer Xperience's entire visual identity runs through a <code>:root</code> block at the top of <code>style.css</code>:</p>
    <pre><code>:root {
  --bg: #0a0a0b;
  --card: #1a1a1f;
  --border: #3a3a4a;
  --accent: #00d4aa;
  --text: #e8e8f0;
      }</code></pre>
    <p>Every color, spacing value, radius, and font size used anywhere else in the stylesheet references one of these &mdash; change <code>--accent</code> once, and every link, focus ring, and highlight on the entire site updates together. That's the payoff of variables: one source of truth instead of the same value copy-pasted (and inevitably drifting out of sync) in fifty places. Variables declared on <code>:root</code> are available everywhere; declaring one on a specific selector instead scopes it, and anything nested inside, to just that element and its children.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="module-4-2">
    <h3>Calc and Math Functions</h3>
    <p><code>calc()</code> mixes units in a single expression &mdash; <code>width: calc(100% - 40px)</code>, for instance, for "fill the space, minus a fixed gutter." <code>min()</code> and <code>max()</code> pick the smaller or larger of a list of values, useful for a value that should flex but never shrink below (or grow past) a floor or ceiling; <code>clamp(min, preferred, max)</code> combines both into one line, commonly used for fluid typography that scales with the viewport but stays within sane bounds.</p>
    <p>Developer Xperience currently handles sizing through its fixed variable scale rather than <code>calc()</code>/<code>clamp()</code> &mdash; a perfectly valid choice for a design with a small number of defined breakpoints, though <code>clamp()</code> is worth reaching for if the type scale ever needs to flex continuously instead of jumping at breakpoints.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="module-4-3">
    <h3>Subgrid</h3>
    <p>Subgrid lets a nested grid inherit its parent's row or column tracks instead of defining its own &mdash; <code>grid-template-columns: subgrid</code> on a child grid item makes its own children line up with the parent grid's columns. It's most useful when independent components (cards in a row, fields in a form) need to align to a shared grid even though they don't share a parent element that's easy to restructure. It's a relatively new addition to CSS Grid &mdash; Module 8 covers the mechanics and edge cases in more depth.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="module-4-4">
    <h3>Gap and Spacing</h3>
    <p><code>gap</code> (with <code>row-gap</code> and <code>column-gap</code> for independent control) spaces out Grid and Flexbox children directly, without the classic margin trick of adding margin to every item and then subtracting it back off the container's edge. It's already doing exactly that job in <code>.main-navigation</code> and <code>.module-nav-list</code> on this page. Unlike margin, <code>gap</code> only affects space between items, never the outer edge, and it doesn't participate in the box model the way margin does &mdash; so it can't collapse unexpectedly the way adjacent margins sometimes do.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="module-4-5">
    <h3>Column Balance</h3>
    <p>The multi-column layout module (<code>column-count</code>, <code>column-width</code>, <code>column-gap</code>) flows text into newspaper-style columns &mdash; distinct from Grid or Flexbox, which arrange discrete elements rather than reflowing a block of text. <code>column-fill: balance</code> (the default) tries to even out content across columns rather than filling the first column completely before starting the next. It's a niche tool today &mdash; most modern layouts reach for Grid instead &mdash; but still the right choice for long-form text that genuinely benefits from a print-like column flow.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="module-4-7">
    <h3>Sample Code</h3>
    <p>Variables, math functions, and gap working together:</p>
    <pre><code>:root {
  --bg: #0a0a0b;
  --card: #1a1a1f;
  --accent: #00d4aa;
  --space: 1rem;
}

.card {
  background: var(--card);
  padding: var(--space);
  margin-bottom: calc(var(--space) * 2);
}

/* Fluid type: scales with the viewport, clamped between 1rem and 1.4rem */
h1 {
  font-size: clamp(1rem, 0.8rem + 2vw, 1.4rem);
}

/* gap spaces flex/grid children without margin hacks */
.toolbar {
  display: flex;
  gap: var(--space);
      }</code></pre>
  </section>
  <section id="module-4-6">
    <h3>Summary</h3>
    <p>Module 4 introduced tools for layouts that need to stay consistent and maintainable at scale: variables for a single source of truth, <code>calc()</code>/<code>min()</code>/<code>max()</code>/<code>clamp()</code> for flexible sizing, subgrid for aligned nested layouts, <code>gap</code> for clean spacing, and multi-column for text-heavy content. Module 5 shifts from individual techniques to the bigger picture &mdash; designing systems that hold up across devices, browsers, and users.</p>
