<article id="module5" class="card">
  <h2>Module 5: Design Systems</h2>
  <section id="module-5-1">
    <h3>Responsive Design Patterns</h3>
    <p>A handful of layout patterns cover most responsive needs: a card grid that reflows its column count with <code>auto-fit</code>/<code>minmax()</code> (exactly the technique behind this page's own module navigation), a navigation bar that collapses from a horizontal row into a stacked or off-canvas menu on narrow screens, and fluid typography that scales gently between a minimum and maximum size. Recognizing these patterns in the wild &mdash; and reusing Developer Xperience's own solutions where they fit &mdash; is usually faster than reinventing a responsive layout from first principles each time.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="module-5-2">
    <h3>Mobile-First Approach</h3>
    <p>Mobile-first means writing base CSS for the smallest screen, then layering on complexity for larger ones with <code>min-width</code> media queries &mdash; the opposite direction from starting wide and cutting down. It's worth noticing that Developer Xperience's own breakpoints (<code>@media (max-width: 768px)</code>) actually work the other way: base styles target desktop, and the media queries adjust downward for smaller screens. That's a <strong>desktop-first</strong> approach, and it's a completely legitimate choice, especially for a content-heavy site like this one. Mobile-first tends to pay off most when a design is complex enough that starting simple genuinely prevents rework later.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="module-5-3">
    <h3>Cross-Browser Compatibility</h3>
    <p>Not every browser ships every CSS feature at the same time. <code>@supports</code> lets a stylesheet test for a feature and provide a fallback if it's missing:</p>
    <pre><code>@supports (display: grid) {
  .layout { display: grid; }
      }</code></pre>
    <p>Caniuse.com is the standard reference for checking real-world support before relying on a newer feature. Vendor prefixes (<code>-webkit-</code>, <code>-moz-</code>) still show up for a handful of properties, though they're far less necessary than a decade ago now that browsers have converged on shared standards. A CSS reset or normalize layer &mdash; like the box-sizing and margin/padding reset at the top of <code>style.css</code> &mdash; removes most of the baseline inconsistency between browsers before your own styles even apply.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="module-5-4">
    <h3>Accessibility in CSS</h3>
    <p>CSS accessibility work shows up in several places already visible on this page: sufficient color contrast between text and background (WCAG recommends at least 4.5:1 for normal body text), visible focus indicators (this site's <code>:focus-visible</code> outlines), and respecting reduced-motion preferences:</p>
    <pre><code>@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  *, *::before, *::after {
    transition-duration: 0.01ms !important;
    animation-duration: 0.01ms !important;
    transform: none !important;
  }
      }</code></pre>
    <p>which is exactly what <code>style.css</code> does globally &mdash; anyone with that OS-level preference set gets all transitions and animations effectively instant, rather than motion that could cause discomfort. Color should also never be the only signal for meaning: a red border alone doesn't tell a colorblind user a field failed validation, so pair it with an icon or text too.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="module-5-5">
    <h3>Performance Optimization</h3>
    <p>CSS affects performance in a few concrete ways: file size (minifying and compressing the stylesheet for production), selector cost (a simple class selector is cheaper for the browser to match than a long descendant chain), and render-blocking (<code>&lt;link rel="stylesheet"&gt;</code> in the <code>&lt;head&gt;</code>, as every page on this site does, blocks first paint until it loads &mdash; worth it for avoiding a flash of unstyled content, but a reason to keep stylesheets lean). Properties like <code>box-shadow</code>, <code>filter</code>, and animating anything other than <code>transform</code>/<code>opacity</code> are comparatively expensive to repaint; reach for <code>will-change</code> sparingly, and only on elements about to animate.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="module-5-7">
    <h3>Sample Code</h3>
    <p>System-level patterns: responsive reflow, feature fallback, and reduced motion:</p>
    <pre><code>/* Responsive card grid - column count reflows on its own */
.card-grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(220px, 1fr));
  gap: 1.5rem;
}

/* Graceful fallback when a feature is missing */
@supports (display: grid) {
  .layout { display: grid; }
}

/* Respect users who prefer less motion */
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  *, *::before, *::after {
    transition-duration: 0.01ms !important;
    animation-duration: 0.01ms !important;
  }
      }</code></pre>
  </section>
  <section id="module-5-6">
    <h3>Summary</h3>
    <p>Module 5 zoomed out from individual properties to system-level thinking: recognizable responsive patterns, a deliberate mobile-first-or-desktop-first choice, cross-browser fallbacks, accessibility as a first-class concern rather than an afterthought, and performance-conscious CSS. Module 6 puts all of it into practice with three real, buildable projects.</p>
