<article id="module6" class="card">
  <h2>Module 6: Practical Projects</h2>
  <section id="module-6-1">
    <h3>Build a Landing Page</h3>
    <p>Build a standalone landing page from scratch: a header with navigation, a hero section with a headline and call-to-action button, a grid of three or four feature cards, and a footer. Use Flexbox for the navigation, Grid for the feature cards, and at least one media query so it holds up on a phone screen. For an extra rep that connects straight back to this platform: try rebuilding this page's own header &mdash; banner image, heading, and navigation &mdash; from an empty stylesheet, without looking at <code>style.css</code>, then compare your version against the real one afterward.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="module-6-2">
    <h3>Create a Form</h3>
    <p>Build a contact or sign-up form: labeled text, email, and select inputs, a textarea, and a submit button. Style clear <code>:focus-visible</code> states (Module 3 covered exactly this), a <code>:disabled</code> state, and a distinct style for an invalid field using <code>:invalid</code> or a <code>.error</code> class. Group related fields with <code>&lt;fieldset&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;legend&gt;</code> so the structure holds up for screen readers as well as sighted users.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="module-6-3">
    <h3>Newsletter Subscription</h3>
    <p>Build a compact newsletter signup: an email input and a submit button, styled as a single unit &mdash; an inline form is a natural small Flexbox exercise. Add a <code>:focus-visible</code> state on the input, a hover/active state on the button, and a simple "Subscribed!" confirmation state you can toggle by adding a class &mdash; no backend required, just practice swapping styles based on a state change.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="module-6-5">
    <h3>Sample Code</h3>
    <p>A project skeleton that combines layout, states, and responsiveness:</p>
    <pre><code>/* Landing page header - flexbox navigation */
.nav {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  gap: 1rem;
  justify-content: space-between;
}

/* Feature cards - responsive grid */
.features {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(220px, 1fr));
  gap: 1.5rem;
}

/* Form field states */
input:focus-visible {
  outline: 2px solid var(--accent);
  outline-offset: 2px;
}

input:disabled {
  opacity: 0.5;
}

input:invalid {
  border-color: #e5484d;
}

/* Mobile: single column */
@media (max-width: 600px) {
  .features {
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
  }
      }</code></pre>
  </section>
  <section id="module-6-4">
    <h3>Summary</h3>
    <p>These three projects put Modules 1&ndash;5 to work end to end: layout, typography, color, states, and responsiveness, all in service of something a portfolio can actually show off. Module 7 turns to how CSS at this scale gets organized so it stays maintainable as a project grows.</p>
