<article id="module7" class="card">
  <h2>Module 7: CSS Architecture</h2>
  <section id="module-7-1">
    <h3>BEM Methodology</h3>
    <p>BEM &mdash; Block, Element, Modifier &mdash; is a class-naming convention: <code>.block</code>, <code>.block__element</code>, <code>.block--modifier</code> (for example, <code>.card__title</code> or <code>.button--disabled</code>). The goal is predictability: a class name alone tells you what a piece of UI is and how it relates to its parent, without needing to trace the CSS to find out. Developer Xperience's own stylesheet doesn't follow strict BEM &mdash; it favors descriptive component names instead, like <code>.module-nav-list</code> and <code>.submodule-aside</code> &mdash; which is a completely valid alternative as long as a project stays consistent about whichever convention it picks. BEM specifically earns its keep most on larger teams, where a shared, rigid naming pattern prevents accidental class collisions.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="module-7-2">
    <h3>Scalable CSS Patterns</h3>
    <p>Beyond BEM, larger projects often organize stylesheets by layer. ITCSS, for instance, orders rules from broadest to most specific &mdash; global resets and variables, then generic element styles, then components, then utility overrides &mdash; so specificity naturally increases as you read down the file, which is roughly the same order <code>style.css</code> already follows: root variables and reset first, then base element styles, then components like <code>.card</code>, then responsive overrides at the bottom. SMACSS categorizes rules by purpose (base, layout, module, state, theme) instead. Neither is mandatory for a project this size, but the underlying instinct &mdash; group related rules, keep specificity trending in one direction &mdash; scales down just as well as it scales up.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="module-7-3">
    <h3>CSS Organization</h3>
    <p>Splitting a stylesheet into partials (<code>_variables.css</code>, <code>_layout.css</code>, <code>_components.css</code>) and combining them with <code>@import</code> or a build step keeps individual files small and focused as a project grows. <code>style.css</code> is still a single file, organized instead with clear numbered section comments (<code>/* 1. Color palette */</code>, <code>/* 2. Base reset &amp; typography */</code>, <code>/* 11. Responsive layout */</code>, and so on) &mdash; a lighter-weight version of the same idea, and a reasonable choice until the file grows large enough that scrolling between sections becomes genuinely painful.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="module-7-4">
    <h3>Preprocessors Overview</h3>
    <p>Preprocessors like Sass extend CSS with variables, nesting, and mixins that get compiled down to plain CSS before shipping. Modern CSS has since absorbed some of that directly into the language, without a build step: custom properties (Module 4) replace most of what Sass variables did, and native CSS nesting &mdash; supported in all current browsers &mdash; replaces basic Sass-style nesting. <code>style.css</code> already uses native nesting, not Sass, for the contact form button near the bottom of the file:</p>
    <pre><code>.contact-form button {
  background: var(--accent);

  &amp;:hover {
    background: var(--accent-hover);
  }
      }</code></pre>
    <p>That <code>&amp;</code> refers back to the parent selector, exactly like it would in Sass &mdash; except this compiles to nothing, because the browser understands it natively. Preprocessors still earn their keep for features native CSS doesn't have yet, like mixins with real logic or math beyond <code>calc()</code>.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="module-7-5">
    <h3>Modern Workflow</h3>
    <p>A typical modern CSS workflow adds tooling around the stylesheet itself: Autoprefixer inserts vendor prefixes automatically based on a target browser list, a bundler (Vite, webpack) minifies and optimizes CSS for production, and a dev server with hot module reload reflects style changes instantly without a full page refresh. Utility-first frameworks (Tailwind) and CSS-in-JS (styled-components) are alternative philosophies entirely &mdash; trading a hand-written stylesheet like <code>style.css</code> for styles generated or colocated differently &mdash; worth knowing about, not necessarily worth adopting for every project.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="module-7-7">
    <h3>Sample Code</h3>
    <p>BEM naming and native CSS nesting:</p>
    <pre><code>/* BEM: Block__Element--Modifier */
.card__title {
  font-weight: 700;
}

.card--featured {
  border-color: var(--accent);
}

.button--disabled {
  opacity: 0.5;
  pointer-events: none;
}

/* Native CSS nesting - no preprocessor required */
.card {
  border-radius: 8px;

  &amp;:hover {
    box-shadow: 0 12px 40px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.55);
  }
      }</code></pre>
  </section>
  <section id="module-7-6">
    <h3>Summary</h3>
    <p>Module 7 covered how CSS stays maintainable past the first few hundred lines: naming conventions like BEM, layered organization like ITCSS/SMACSS, sensible file structure, and where native CSS now overlaps with what preprocessors used to be required for. Modules 8 and 9 return to Grid and Flexbox for a deeper, more technical pass now that the architectural vocabulary is in place.</p>
