<article id="module8" class="card">
  <h2>Module 8: CSS Grid Deep Dive</h2>
  <section id="module-8-1">
    <h3>Explicit vs Implicit Grid</h3>
    <p>The <strong>explicit</strong> grid is whatever <code>grid-template-columns</code> and <code>grid-template-rows</code> define directly. The <strong>implicit</strong> grid is what the browser generates automatically for items that don't fit inside those explicit tracks, sized by <code>grid-auto-rows</code>/<code>grid-auto-columns</code>, which default to <code>auto</code>. In practice: define the explicit grid for the structure you know in advance, and let the implicit grid absorb items you didn't plan for exactly, like an unpredictable number of cards in a list.</p>
    <pre><code>.gallery {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
  grid-auto-rows: minmax(150px, auto);
      }</code></pre>
  </section>
  <section id="module-8-2">
    <h3>Named Lines</h3>
    <p>Grid lines can be named directly in the template definition &mdash; <code>grid-template-columns: [sidebar-start] 250px [sidebar-end content-start] 1fr [content-end]</code> &mdash; and then referenced by name in <code>grid-column: sidebar-start / sidebar-end</code> instead of counting numbered lines. Named <code>grid-template-areas</code> take this further, letting you sketch the layout as a small text grid of area names and place each item by name &mdash; often the most readable way to define a page-level layout.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="module-8-3">
    <h3>Auto-placement</h3>
    <p>When grid items don't specify an exact position, <code>grid-auto-flow</code> controls how the browser slots them in automatically: <code>row</code> (the default, filling row by row) or <code>column</code>. Adding <code>dense</code> (<code>grid-auto-flow: row dense</code>) lets the browser backfill gaps left by irregularly-sized items, at the cost of possibly reordering content visually away from its source order &mdash; worth using carefully, since screen readers still follow DOM order regardless of where dense packing visually places something.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="module-8-4">
    <h3>Subgrid Details</h3>
    <p>Where Module 4 introduced subgrid conceptually, here are the mechanics: <code>grid-template-columns: subgrid</code> on a nested grid item tells it to adopt its parent's column tracks instead of defining new ones &mdash; and critically, it can still add its own rows independently while inheriting only the columns (or vice versa with <code>grid-template-rows: subgrid</code>). This solves a specific, common problem plain nested grids can't: aligning content across separate card components, so every card's title, body, and footer line up on the same horizontal lines even though each card is a separate grid in the DOM. Browser support is solid in current versions of all major engines, but still worth a quick <code>@supports (grid-template-columns: subgrid)</code> check if a layout needs to degrade gracefully on older browsers.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="module-8-5">
    <h3>Grid and Accessibility</h3>
    <p>Grid controls visual placement only &mdash; <code>order</code>, <code>grid-row</code>, and <code>grid-column</code> can display content in a different position than it appears in the HTML, but screen readers and keyboard tab order still follow the underlying DOM order. If a grid layout visually reorders content for design reasons, double-check that the reading order still makes sense with CSS disabled entirely, or with a keyboard alone &mdash; a mismatch between visual and DOM order is one of the more common accessibility bugs Grid-heavy layouts introduce.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="module-8-7">
    <h3>Sample Code</h3>
    <p>Explicit tracks, named lines, and grid areas:</p>
    <pre><code>/* Explicit + implicit grid */
.gallery {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
  grid-auto-rows: minmax(150px, auto);
}

/* Named lines */
.layout {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: [sidebar] 250px [content] 1fr [end];
}

.sidebar {
  grid-column: sidebar / content;
}

.main {
  grid-column: content / end;
}

/* Named areas - layout sketched as text */
.page {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-areas:
    "header header"
    "sidebar main";
}

.site-header { grid-area: header; }
.site-sidebar { grid-area: sidebar; }
      .site-main { grid-area: main; }</code></pre>
  </section>
  <section id="module-8-6">
    <h3>Summary</h3>
    <p>Module 8 went deep on Grid specifically: explicit vs. implicit tracks, named lines and areas, auto-placement, subgrid's real mechanics, and the accessibility trade-offs of purely visual reordering. Module 9 gives Flexbox the same treatment.</p>
