  </section>
</article>
<hr />
<article id="module9" class="card">
  <h2>Module 9: Flexbox Mastery</h2>
  <section id="module-9-1">
    <h3>Main Axis vs Cross Axis</h3>
    <p>Every flex container has a <strong>main axis</strong>, set by <code>flex-direction</code> (<code>row</code> by default, or <code>column</code>), and a <strong>cross axis</strong>, perpendicular to it. <code>justify-content</code> always aligns along the main axis; <code>align-items</code>/<code>align-self</code> always align along the cross axis &mdash; the same properties, but which axis they affect flips depending on <code>flex-direction</code>. In the header banner (<code>flex-direction: row</code>, the default), <code>align-items: center</code> centers the image, heading, and more-options button vertically &mdash; switch that same container to <code>flex-direction: column</code> and the exact same <code>align-items: center</code> would instead center them horizontally.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="module-9-2">
    <h3>Alignment and Justification</h3>
    <p><code>justify-content</code> distributes space along the main axis: <code>flex-start</code>, <code>flex-end</code>, <code>center</code>, <code>space-between</code> (edges flush, gaps even between), <code>space-around</code> (equal space around each item, so edge gaps look half-size), and <code>space-evenly</code> (every gap, including the edges, truly equal). <code>align-items</code> sets the default cross-axis alignment for every item; <code>align-self</code> overrides it for one item individually. <code>stretch</code>, the default, fills the entire cross-axis space unless an item has an explicit size.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="module-9-3">
    <h3>Order Property</h3>
    <p><code>order</code> changes an item's visual position without touching the DOM &mdash; every flex item defaults to <code>order: 0</code>, and lower values shift earlier, higher values shift later. It's genuinely useful for small responsive reflows, like moving a sidebar above content on mobile, but exactly like Grid's visual reordering in Module 8, it never changes DOM order &mdash; screen readers and keyboard tabbing still follow the markup, so <code>order</code> should reorder appearance only, never be relied on to fix a confusing reading order.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="module-9-4">
    <h3>Nested Flexbox</h3>
    <p>A flex item can itself be a flex container for its own children, which is how most real interfaces are actually built &mdash; a page-level flex row containing a card, where the card is internally a flex column for its own title, body, and footer. Keep an eye on how many levels deep this goes; three or four nested flex contexts are usually still readable, but past that it's often a sign the layout wants Grid instead, which can express a two-dimensional structure in one container rather than several nested one-dimensional ones.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="module-9-5">
    <h3>Flexbox Debugging</h3>
    <p>Browser DevTools include a dedicated Flexbox inspector &mdash; a small badge next to any element using <code>display: flex</code> in the Elements/Inspector panel &mdash; that overlays the main and cross axes directly on the page and lists the computed values for every alignment property. The most common Flexbox surprise is an item refusing to shrink below its content's natural size; the fix is usually <code>min-width: 0</code> (or <code>min-height: 0</code> in a column layout) on the item, which overrides the default <code>min-width: auto</code> that otherwise fights against <code>flex-shrink</code>.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="module-9-7">
    <h3>Sample Code</h3>
    <p>Axes, alignment, and order:</p>
    <pre><code>/* Row direction: main axis is horizontal */
.nav {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: row;            /* main axis: left to right */
  justify-content: space-between; /* main axis alignment */
  align-items: center;            /* cross axis alignment */
}

/* Column direction: main axis is vertical */
.stack {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  justify-content: center;
}

/* Per-item override */
.cta {
  align-self: flex-end;
}

/* Visual reorder without touching the DOM */
.sidebar {
  order: -1; /* appears first; DOM order unchanged */
}

/* Fix: let flex items shrink below their content size */
.elastic-item {
  min-width: 0;
      }</code></pre>
  </section>
  <section id="module-9-6">
    <h3>Summary</h3>
    <p>Module 9 went deep on Flexbox: the main/cross axis relationship, the full alignment vocabulary, <code>order</code> and its accessibility caveat, nested flex contexts, and a real debugging fix for the most common Flexbox surprise. Between Modules 8 and 9, Grid and Flexbox should both feel like reliable, well-understood tools rather than trial and error.</p>
    <table border="1">
      <caption>Flexbox Mastery at a Glance</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Concept</th>
          <th scope="col">Category</th>
          <th scope="col">What It Does</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Main vs cross axis</td>
          <td>Model</td>
          <td>flex-direction picks the main axis; alignment flips with it</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>justify-content</td>
          <td>Alignment</td>
          <td>Distributes space along the main axis</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>align-items / align-self</td>
          <td>Alignment</td>
          <td>Cross-axis alignment for all items or a single item</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>order</td>
          <td>Placement</td>
          <td>Visual reordering only - DOM order stays for screen readers</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Nested flexbox</td>
          <td>Structure</td>
          <td>Flex items that are themselves flex containers</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>min-width: 0</td>
          <td>Debugging</td>
          <td>Lets items shrink below their content's natural size</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>
</article>
