<article id="module10" class="card">
  <h2>Module 10: Final Projects &amp; Review</h2>
  <section id="module-10-1">
    <h3>Complete Web Page Redesign</h3>
    <p>Pick an existing page &mdash; Developer Xperience's own <code>index.html</code> is a good candidate &mdash; and redesign it using everything from Modules 1&ndash;9: Grid or Flexbox for layout, custom properties for a cohesive palette, responsive breakpoints, accessible focus states, and at least one thoughtful animation or transition. Redesigning something real and already familiar, rather than a from-scratch mockup, makes it far easier to judge whether the result is actually better, not just different.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="module-10-2">
    <h3>CSS Portfolio Layout</h3>
    <p>Build a personal portfolio page: a Grid-based overall structure, project cards with a hover effect, and a dark-mode alternative using CSS variables and <code>prefers-color-scheme</code> &mdash; the same variable-driven theming technique from Module 4, applied to a light/dark toggle instead of a single fixed palette. This is a strong candidate for the actual portfolio project you're building alongside this course.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="module-10-3">
    <h3>Responsive Admin Dashboard</h3>
    <p>Build a dashboard layout: sidebar navigation, a main content area, and a data area (a table or a few chart placeholders are fine), using Grid for the overall page structure and Flexbox for aligning items within each region. Make the sidebar collapse to icons-only, or an off-canvas drawer, below a chosen breakpoint, and make sure every interactive element still has a visible <code>:focus-visible</code> state once it does.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="module-10-4">
    <h3>Accessible Component Library</h3>
    <p>Build a small library of reusable components &mdash; button, input, card, and a simple modal &mdash; with consistent, documented states for hover, focus, active, and disabled on each. Pick one naming convention, BEM or Developer Xperience's own descriptive style from Module 7, and apply it consistently. A documented component library is one of the more portfolio-relevant projects in this whole track: concrete proof of both CSS range and of thinking in reusable systems rather than one-off pages.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="module-10-5">
    <h3>Final Exam Project</h3>
    <p>Choose, or propose, one comprehensive project that pulls together layout, responsiveness, accessibility, performance-conscious CSS, and a clear architectural convention, and build it end to end. This is the project worth polishing enough to actually link from a portfolio or resume &mdash; treat it as the capstone it's meant to be, not just one more exercise.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="module-10-7">
    <h3>Sample Code</h3>
    <p>A capstone dashboard skeleton combining variables, Grid, Flexbox, and theming:</p>
    <pre><code>:root {
  --sidebar-width: 240px;
  --accent: #00d4aa;
}

/* Page structure - grid with a sidebar region */
.dashboard {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: var(--sidebar-width) 1fr;
  grid-template-areas:
    "sidebar main";
  min-height: 100vh;
}

.dashboard__sidebar { grid-area: sidebar; }
.dashboard__main { grid-area: main; }

/* Dark mode via variables */
@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
  :root {
    --bg: #0a0a0b;
    --text: #e8e8f0;
  }
}

/* Collapse the sidebar below the breakpoint */
@media (max-width: 768px) {
  .dashboard {
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    grid-template-areas: "main";
  }
      }</code></pre>
  </section>
  <section id="module-10-6">
    <h3>Summary and Next Steps</h3>
    <p>That's the CSS roadmap end to end: fundamentals, layout, interactivity, advanced techniques, systems-level thinking, hands-on projects, architecture, and a deep pass on both Grid and Flexbox. From here, the natural next step is JavaScript, bringing behavior to the structure and style this track and the HTML track already cover.</p>
